 Human(oatmeal) = Adult(oatmeal)  Child(oatmeal) false = false  false false = false true X 
Whoa, how did _true_ pop out of that? Simply because the premise was false, and so all bets were off. We effectively said " _if_ a bowl of oatmeal is human, _then_ it will either be an adult or a child. But it's not, so never mind." Put another way, the bowl of oatmeal did _not_ turn out to be a counterexample, and so we're confident claiming that this expression is true "for _all_ h": h. 
The other kind of quantifier is called the **existential quantifier**. As its name suggests, it asserts the _existence_ of something. We write it "" and pronounce it "there exists." For example, 
 x HasGovernor(x) 
asserts that there is _at least one_ state that has a governor. This doesn't tell us how _many_ states this is true for, and in fact despite their name, quantifiers really aren't very good at "quantifying" things for us, at least numerically. As of 2008, the statement 
