That was pretty tedious. But converting back and forth from binary to _hex_ is a snap. That's because 16 is exactly 24 , and so one hex digit is exactly equal to four binary digits. This isn't the case with base 10, where one decimal digit is equal to three binary digits... _plus_ a little extra. This "not quite a whole number of digits" thing is what makes converting from decimal to binary (or decimal to hex, for that matter) so awkward. 
